Introduction
During my six-month internship at BEUMER Group, within the Airport Engineering department, I worked on the design and development of a lightweight SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol) agent for the Autoca embedded controller system.
The Autoca system is part of industrial airport logistics, where multiple automated carts operate within a network. Monitoring the state of these devices in real-time is critical for maintaining performance, debugging issues, and ensuring system reliability.
The goal of my project was to enable remote monitoring capability by implementing a simplified SNMPv1 agent, allowing external monitoring tools (such as PRTG) to query real-time device information over a network.

Project Objective
The primary objective of the project was to:

Develop a lightweight SNMP agent suitable for embedded systems
Enable UDP-based communication on port 161
Provide real-time system information such as device identity and heartbeat
Integrate SNMP into an existing embedded RTOS environment
Ensure low memory and CPU usage

Unlike full-scale SNMP servers, the focus was on a minimal and optimized solution specifically designed for resource-constrained systems.

Understanding the System
Before implementation, I first built a strong understanding of:

SNMP architecture (Manager–Agent model)
MIB (Management Information Base)
OIDs (Object Identifiers)
UDP communication principles
BER (Basic Encoding Rules) for packet encoding

This foundation was essential to design a system that correctly communicates with external SNMP managers.

System Architecture
The system I worked on was modular and consisted of the following key components:
1. SNMP Agent
The SNMP agent runs inside the Autoca device and is responsible for:

Receiving SNMP requests
Processing OIDs
Responding with corresponding values

2. SNMP Manager
An external system (e.g., PRTG) sends SNMP GET requests to retrieve system data.
3. MIB (Data Storage)
A simple database that stores:
OID → Value

Example:
1.3.6.1.2.1.1.5.0 → "autoca 103"


Implementation Details
1. MIB Implementation (snmp_mib.c)
One of the core parts of my work was building a simple in-memory MIB database.

Implemented using a fixed-size array (32 entries)
Each entry stores:

OID (string)
Value (string)



Key Features:

Initialization of MIB structure
Adding and updating OID entries
Searching and retrieving values
Handling cases where OIDs do not exist

To ensure safety, I implemented a control flag (g_bReady) to prevent access before initialization or after shutdown.

2. UDP Communication (snmp_udp.c)
I implemented the network layer responsible for SNMP communication.

Listens on UDP port 161
Receives incoming SNMP packets
Sends responses back to the manager

Key Learning:

UDP is connectionless and lightweight, which makes it ideal for embedded SNMP communication.
Unlike TCP, no connection setup is required, reducing overhead.


3. SNMP Packet Processing (snmp_v1.c)
This module handles conversion between raw network packets and readable data.
Responsibilities:

Extract OIDs from incoming SNMP requests
Build SNMP response messages
Handle error responses for invalid OIDs

This was one of the most technically challenging parts of the project.

4. BER Encoding / Decoding
SNMP packets are not sent as readable text; instead, they use BER (Basic Encoding Rules).
I worked on understanding and using BER to:

Decode incoming requests into readable OIDs
Encode response values back into byte format

Concept:
BER follows:
[Tag][Length][Value]

Example:

Tag → Type of data
Length → Size
Value → Actual information

Learning BER significantly improved my understanding of low-level networking protocols.

5. Data Generation (snmp_tiny.c)
I implemented logic to populate the MIB with relevant system information.
Startup Initialization:

System description
Device name
Device location

Runtime Updates:

Heartbeat value updated every 2 seconds

This allows monitoring systems to track whether the device is active.

6. Multithreading Design
To ensure real-time performance, I worked with a multi-threaded architecture:

Thread 1: Periodic heartbeat updates
Thread 2: Continuous network listening

This design allows the system to:

Respond to requests instantly
Update system values simultaneously


